Active disassembly uses smart materials — primarily shape memory alloys (SMAs) and shape memory polymers (SMPs) — as functional fasteners that are activated by an external stimulus (usually heat). When the product reaches end of use, it is placed in a controlled environment where the stimulus is applied, causing the smart material fasteners to change shape and release the connections simultaneously. This enables rapid, automated separation of components without manual labour or destructive force. SMAs activate at specific temperatures (austenite finish temperature), while SMPs lose rigidity or change form when heated above their glass transition temperature.